itude  and phase coordinates and there's this  really beautiful connection to these  action angle coordinates from classical  mechanics that you can read about in his  archive paper from 2017 so that was kind  of a fun connection we made you know we  showed this to Igor and he pointed out  if we map it in this coordinate system  we basically get action angle  coordinates for this pendulum which is  really really nice and satisfying  because that connects us back to this  kind of hundred years of or more than  100 years of classical mechanics and  again it all relies on this auxilary  network of parameterizing this or else  we wouldn't have been able to get to  eigenfunctions we would have had all of  these harmonics plus or minus two I  Omega plus or minus three I and I got  four you know all of those harmonics  would have been necessary here okay so  she's also applied this to more rich  systems like the flow past a cylinder  which also has this continuous  deformation of my eigenvalues as I  increase or decrease the energy level of  the system so she finds these  eigenfunctions here that mapped me into  a linear coordinate system so it's  this method scales because it is relying  on the power and flexibility of this  this deep learning architecture okay so  those are just a couple of the many  approaches people are starting to play  with for continuous spectrum dynamical  systems so it turns out maybe maybe  other people knew this but I didn't  appreciate even two years ago how  ubiquitous the continuous spectrum is  and dynamical systems most of the  systems of interest to me that I'm  interested in have a continuous spectrum  they don't just have discrete eigen  values they have this continuous  deformation of the eigenvalues over you  know in energy or control inputs and so  I think this is only going to become  more and more important to start  thinking of how our Koopman  representation applies to these  continuous spectrum systems and  currently I think two 